  "wasmtime": "wasmtime 47.0.1",
  "target_seconds": 0.5,
  "method": "AIHC built with aihc build (Nix build of the commit above) into a build root per cell, Wasm precompiled with wasmtime compile -C cache=n and run with -S cli -S http --allow-precompiled. Other sessions loaded the machine (load average 8 to 65), so sizes were fitted on the child process's CPU time (user + system), which stayed within about 10% of wall time on a quiet machine; cpu_seconds and wall_seconds are each the minimum of three complete runs (one run for the frontend cells). Benchmarks with a --print or --modules mode were searched without rebuilding; the others were scaled linearly and rebuilt. Every checksum, digest and tally was taken from a GHC 9.14.1 build (SHA digests from Python's hashlib) and matched AIHC's output.",
  "notes": {
    "microhs-self-compile": "Not recalibrated: its input (all of MicroHs) is fixed.",
    "aihc-resolve-bench": "Cells process a dependency-closed prefix of the corpus modules; the smallest, the 14-module wired primitive cycle, exceeds the target in every cell but native/LLVM O1 and O2.",
    "aihc-tc-bench": "As for aihc-resolve-bench. LLVM and Wasm O0 were not timed here: on the 14-module minimum they took 102 and 163 CPU seconds earlier in the session and exceed the 120-second process timeout.",
    "fgl-queries": "LLVM and Wasm O0 run one query per family, the smallest input.",
    "arithmoi-factorise": "O0 and Wasm Os lists leave out the cases that reach the elliptic curve method, whose prime sieve alone exceeds the target there.",
    "aihc-parser-stackage": "Wasm O2 at 168 modules traps ('cannot leave component instance', an AIHC Wasm runtime failure); 300 modules runs.",
    "aihc-cpp-stackage": "Module counts below about 30 are lumpy: each count is an even stride through the pool, so the module set changes with the count."
  },
